 "summary": "Establishes a small business tax credit for the employment of disabled persons.",
 "title": "Establishes a small business tax credit for the employment of disabled persons",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A1369",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A1369-2017",
     "printNo": "A1369",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 269\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 1369-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the tax law, in relation to establishing a small\n      business tax credit for the employment of  disabled  persons;  and\n      providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration there-\n      of\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would provide a non-refundable $5,000 tax credit to corpo-\nrations and employers that have 100 employees or less, and employ one or\nmore disabled persons who work 35 hours or  more  per  week  and  remain\nemployed with the corporations or businesses for twelve months or more.\n \n  At  the outset, this bill has several fundamental flaws that undermine\nits goal, compromise the state's tax treatment and aligns  a  fiscal  to\nthe  State  with no funding to support it. I have promoted programs that\nsupport the employment of individuals with disabilities to  ensure  that\nNew  York is a livable and workable place for all. From the Workers with\nDisabilities Credit and the New York Employment First Initiative to  the\nrecent  launch  of  the  Able New York Initiative, my Office has ensured\nthat New York is a desirable place for disabled individuals  who  desire\nto live and work in this state.\n \n  The  bill's  stated goal to incentivize the employment for individuals\nwith disabilities is a laudable one, but the bill  faces  insurmountable\nlegal,  technical,  and fiscal problems that make it impossible to sign.\nAs written, the employment requirements in this bill could  function  to\nundermine  the bill's goal to \"stimulate employment of disabled persons\"\nbecause the requirement  that  a  newly  hired  disabled  person  remain\nemployed  for twelve months would mean that the employer could not claim\nthe credit for the year in which the  employee  was  hired  unless  that\nemployee  was  hired on January 1st.  Further, the bill would also allow\ncredits for hiring that do not expand the number of disabled  people  in\nthe  workforce.  For example, the bill as written, allows an employer to\nhire a disabled person away from another employer,  and  does  not  have\nsafeguards  in place to prevent the termination and rehiring of the same\nperson in order to trigger a second credit.\n \n  Additionally, although the bill requires  the  employer  claiming  the\ncredit  to  be  a  New York State taxpayer, there is nothing in the bill\nthat would require the  employer's  workforce,  including  the  disabled\nemployees, to live or work in New York State.\n \n  This  bill  would  also jeopardize the federal treatment of New York's\ncorporate taxes as non-discriminatory franchise taxes by allowing corpo-\nrations to avoid paying fixed  dollar  minimums.  Taxpayers  subject  to\nfranchise taxes must pay at least a minimum amount of tax for the privi-\nlege  of doing business in the State. This concept is vital to maintain-\ning these taxes as non-discriminatory franchise taxes. Because this bill\nwould allow corporations to reduce their tax to zero in  any  year,  the\nclassification  of a tax as a franchise tax would be jeopardized, poten-\n \ntially putting at risk millions of dollars of State franchise tax reven-\nue.\n \n  Additionally,  while  the bill would impose an annual aggregate cap of\n$5 million, and require the Department of Taxation and Finance to  allo-\ncate  the  credit  among  taxpayers,  the  bill does not specify how the\nDepartment should allocate the credits. The failure of the  bill  to  be\nprescriptive  in  credit  allocation is in stark contrast to other allo-\ncated credit statutes, which  prescribe  an  application  procedure  and\nstandards  to  determine  whom the credit should be allocated. Neverthe-\nless, any new program with a fiscal impact of this magnitude needs to be\naddressed in the context of State Budget negotiations.\n \n  While I am constrained to veto this bill, I look  forward  to  working\nwith  the Legislature to develop a program that meets this bill's stated\npurpose of incentivizing employment of disabled individuals.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
